Excluded on Solaris due to timing and reporting inconsistency.

This 5-node test asserts the de-duplication invariant at relay D
(D.rcv == 2, D.fwd == 1) by sampling bpstats tallies after the bundle
propagates.  On the Solaris regression host the node-to-node forwarding
pipeline is heavily CPU-starved, so end-to-end propagation is both very
slow and highly variable (observed ~40s to ~2min for the same test), and
bpstats' asynchronous "[x]" stats dump lags behind the live counters.  The
de-duplication itself does occur on Solaris -- run artifacts confirm
D rcv=2, fwd=1 -- but the test cannot reliably read the tallies within a
bounded window, producing spurious failures.  Run this test on Linux, where
it is deterministic.
